<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian front (World War I)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Fronte italiano, Südwestfront</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italian front was one of the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, a series of engagements along the border between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> from 1915 to 1918. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> entered the war on the Entente side to annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>, Istria and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, promised to it in the 1915 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915">Treaty of London</a>. The front bogged down into trench warfare, much of it at high altitudes, and several thousand civilians died in refugee camps. Fighting ended with Italian victory and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> of Villa Giusti on 4 November 1918, and Italy annexed Trento, Trieste, South Tyrol and Istria.</p></div>
